@page "/"
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

<PageTitle>StallTrack</PageTitle>

@*
    The address the office actually types: console.stalltrack.site, with no path.

    There was no route for "/" at all — this file held a commented-out sample page — and the router has no NotFound, so the
    bare domain answered 404. Anyone reaching the portal by its own address was told the page did not exist.

    It carries no markup on purpose. A landing page here would be one more screen between an officer and their work, and it
    would have to be built twice over: signed in and not. The root's only job is to send the caller to the right place.
*@

@code {
    /// <summary>Supplied by <c>CascadingAuthenticationState</c> in <c>Routes.razor</c>.</summary>
    [CascadingParameter] private Task<AuthenticationState>? AuthenticationStateTask { get; set; }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Deliberately not decorated with [Authorize]. Letting the authorize view refuse the page would send every visitor to
        // sign in, including one who is already signed in and simply typed the domain. The decision is made here instead, so
        // both answers come from one place.
        var signedIn = AuthenticationStateTask is not null
            && (await AuthenticationStateTask).User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true;

        // replace: true — the root is a junction, not a destination. Leaving it in history means Back returns here and
        // bounces the caller forward again, which reads as a broken button.
        Navigation.NavigateTo(signedIn ? "/menu" : "/login", replace: true);
    }
}
